# Anna on the Anti-Secession Bill
Tamil title: பிரிவினைத் தடை மசோதா பற்றி அண்ணா?

## Part 1

### Images 1-3

The source introduces this as a Tamil translation of the English speech delivered by Arignar Anna in the Rajya Sabha debate on the Anti-Secession Bill. It says that he spoke about the policy held by himself and the Tamil community he represented, and about fundamental rights.

**Anna:** It seems a strange irony within a tragedy that, immediately after declaring our willingness and consent to seat even the Chinese aggressor at a table for negotiations, we should today be discussing a constitutional amendment intended to give the Government a new legal weapon with which to destroy not an enemy but one who works for an ideal.

I have listened with great care to the many observations made from both sides of the House.

As one who introduced the ideal that you now seek to suppress with the aid of law, I wish to explain its history and character, not merely to restate my purpose but to remove certain misunderstandings concerning our demand.

One honourable member said that Dravidastan was demanded after, or upon seeing, Phizo's demand. Nothing could be further from the truth.

Another member said that such separatist sentiments arose after independence. That comes closer, but is still not the truth. The Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam separated from the Dravidar Kazhagam, an organisation that existed long before independence. When debates arose about the future political structure, the Dravidar Kazhagam, of which I was then General Secretary, announced a plan for a political arrangement in the South. The Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam grew out of that organisation and has continued to explain the ideal in that context. It is therefore not a reaction to the ruling party's acts or omissions. Nor does it have any connection with similar or more dangerous problems elsewhere in the country. Before pouncing upon the problem, I ask honourable members at least to analyse it.

Second, immediately after expressing consent to meet and negotiate with an aggressor, should you not at least try to understand us, as proud people of this country, before prohibiting our propaganda?

Have we fallen so low that you may treat us as political untouchables? Is our demand not important enough for you to try to satisfy our minds and adopt a course the people can accept? Will you not even hear our reasons? Have you made even that attempt?

That is my appeal to this House. Without regard to party affiliation, I ask every member to give this aspect serious attention. Did you meet and consult us? Did the ruling party take the trouble to analyse the problem?

I specify the ruling party because several opposition parties did try to examine it. This morning one member courageously and proudly stated what the Communist Party had said on the issue. Ideals are not connected with electoral arrangements.

Even today, the Madras Congress and the DMK have entered an arrangement concerning the election of the Mayor of Madras. I think the House will be interested to know this. Political friendship is one thing; an ideal is entirely different.

**Bhaurao Dewaji Khobragade, Maharashtra:** Then Congress supports separation.

**Anna:** That is precisely my point. Electoral friendship does not mean abandoning one's ideal.

The Madras Congress has the strength to preserve its ideal, and the Chief Minister of Madras possesses robust confidence.

I do not wish to offer commentary here about the Madras Congress or its Chief Minister. I mention this only to show that electoral friendships may be formed without surrendering ideals. But I ask you to understand, analyse, and examine our ideal.

### Images 4-6

This Bill is now introduced to protect and preserve India's sovereignty and territorial integrity. What danger has arisen to that sovereignty? I do not know, and no one has told me.

Perhaps the Law Minister is preparing some new law and that is why he is absent. Had he been here, he might have turned and asked: "Do you not know that divisive forces exist in the country? Do you not know that we formed the National Integration Committee for this very purpose? Do you not know that we have acted upon its recommendations?"

I know very well that the committee was formed under the chairmanship of the able Dr Sir C. P. Ramaswami Iyer. He is a champion who can vigorously defend India's strength, sovereignty, and territorial integrity. How firm a believer is he? As Dewan of Travancore, he proclaimed Travancore an independent sovereign country and announced an agreement with Pakistan. Fortunately for Congress, he followed a policy of non-alignment, and so you made him chairman. I ask members to examine how this committee worked.

It was charged with recommending how national integration might be achieved, not merely with suppressing separatist propaganda. It also had to discover the best constructive method of achieving integration.

What constructive recommendations did it make? What constructive programmes emerged, other than the prohibitory law born of its recommendations?

The National Integration Committee and its associates toured India, honoured our State with a visit, and consulted people of various political views. But it could not meet the DMK because our State Government had meanwhile given us rooms in Vellore Central Prison. That was the reason it supplied for not meeting us.

Yet if the committee genuinely wished to understand our purpose or contact us, our Organising Secretary N. V. Natarajan remained outside prison, as did Member of Parliament K. Rajaram. It could have met either one.

I will not say that Dr Ramaswami Iyer should have visited us in prison. He has experience sending others there but has never gone himself, and I did not expect him to travel so far to meet us.

We are ordinary people and did not ask a committee of such eminent men to display that degree of generosity. But it could have taken the trouble to contact those outside. I appeal to every member: forget for one moment the terrifying nature of our demand and its supposedly dangerous consequences, and answer this. I do not even require words; a slight smile, a cheerful wink, or a friendly nod will suffice. For ordinary courtesy, and at least for the civility of democracy, should the committee not have contacted our party? It did not. Yet it submitted a report.

The explanation of this Bill says it has been introduced entirely in accordance with the National Integration Committee's recommendation.

The very birth of the Bill is therefore wholly undemocratic. I have troubled your patience only to place this view before you.

I turn to another matter. Ruling-party leaders wrongly say that the Dravidastan demand is dangerous. Until a few months or even weeks ago, many of them admitted they did not understand what we were asking. It is clear only that they do not understand it, yet somehow they understand that it is dangerous. What kind of reason or politics is that? I cannot understand.

I do not remember whether it was in this House or the other House, but the Home Minister said all separatist propaganda would be suppressed if it became uncontrollable or crossed a specified limit. No one asked him to explain, because they assumed he meant propaganda involving unlawful or violent acts. He said this a few months ago. What has happened since then? Have we become hunters collecting skulls and heads? Have we engaged in unlawful activity? No. On the contrary, as soon as Chinese aggression occurred, we offered spontaneous and unreserved cooperation with the war effort.

I am glad the Law Minister is not here now.

**A member:** His Deputy Minister is present.

### Images 7-9

**Anna:** When the leader of our party made this same point in the other House, the Law Minister rose, not with a smile but with a stern look and majestic gestures, and said, "That situation arose because of the Defence of India Act."

As Law Minister, the maker of law, he is entitled to extol its power. But his eagerness to magnify that power drove ordinary courtesy from his mind. I do not ask him to offer the DMK a good-conduct recommendation.

We possess abundant public support; no commendation from a legislature can strengthen it further. I add that his eagerness caused him to forget another important matter. The unity of purpose and national enthusiasm visible today arose from the Prime Minister's ability and noble outlook. That is more powerful than law. Laws exist to prohibit and correct: "Do not do this; do not do that." They do not possess the Prime Minister's ability to win the minds of millions beyond party affiliations.

In emphasising the power of law, the Law Minister could at least have said that today's desire for common action arose from Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru's charm and democratic cultural spirit. I do not know what happens within the Cabinet. But what would an outsider reading the Law Minister's speech think? "Why is the country peaceful? Because of the Defence of India Act! Otherwise everyone would be an enemy of the country, devoid of patriotism, and trouble would have broken out." That is the impression it creates.

I ask the Law Minister to observe proportion when speaking forcefully. The Defence of India Act cannot guard the hearts of a country's people; it can only act as their prison guard. I do not mention the DMK's unreserved support for the war effort because I expect a certificate of good behaviour from the Government, or even goodwill in return. I mention it because it reveals a natural thought, a spontaneously arising inner feeling. Can you not see it? Should it not be allowed to grow naturally with time? Is this Bill manure for that growth? No, it will destroy and irritate that feeling.

Why not allow it to mature naturally and flower? What urgency demands this Bill? Why such haste? That is my particular question. I referred to my support for the war effort only to make you understand this.

As I said, we are ordinary people, but we represent thirty-four lakh voters in a State where fifty lakh voters made Congress the ruling party. I trust I need not argue at length about the difference between fifty lakhs and thirty lakhs.

I assure this House that, if you do not seek to destroy our progress or introduce legal methods to suppress us, we shall be the next ruling party in Madras.

The Honourable Union Minister C. Subramaniam extended us a friendly invitation in his Coimbatore speech: "Abandon separation; I shall welcome you forming the Ministry." Yet you refuse ordinary courtesy and democratic civility to such a party.

You gave us no opportunity to explain our purpose before the National Integration Committee and made no effort to win our confidence.

My respected friend Bhupesh Gupta, leader of the Communist Party, offered a proposal: why should all democratic and national forces not unite to campaign against us? I welcome it. I wish to discover whether the people accept my view or yours. Why run away from such a courageous contest?

I ask Bhupesh Gupta to consider this too: before campaigning against us, would it not be sound political practice to try to change our minds?

**Bhupesh Gupta:** That is what I said; I am trying.

**Anna:** I thank Bhupesh Gupta. I do not know whether his method has failed or has not been applied as much as he wishes, but we see no result.

I ask this House to recommend that the Government establish an all-party consultative committee to debate with us. If we are wrong, correct us. If you have sound reasons, change our minds. Instead, you coerce us.

### Images 10-12

I need not explain to this House, filled with legal experts, that coercion by law is the poorest form of argument. When two ideas compete in the marketplace of public opinion, banning one and placing the law behind the other means retreating from the battle of ideas.

Until the Tiruchengode by-election, what view did our State Congress express?

They said, and it was repeated even in this House, that I stood alone. My friend Bhupesh Gupta said I was solitary and hungry-eyed, though they offered no food to satisfy my hunger. Another member said we had no following in Kerala, Karnataka, or Andhra.

I have never claimed to have changed the minds of people in those sister states or gained gratifying support there. I say only that the same sentiment will arise in those linguistic states.

I have never claimed that people in Waltair, Hyderabad, Mysore, or Trivandrum think exactly as I do. I have not even visited all those places or addressed a meeting in Hyderabad. Why not permit me to go? Why not come with me? In a spirit of competition, I offer this proposal: form an all-party consultative committee, tour the country together, and discover what the country needs.

Change our minds and then say that what I ask is unworthy even of consideration. But do not first bring this Bill and then ask what I intend to do about it.

My friend Bhupesh Gupta said, "We shall go underground." We have always moved openly and have no intention of going underground, but embittered discontent will.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** That is what I said.

**Anna:** Embittered discontent will go underground, and no law or effort can do anything about it. Political philosophy has not yet invented a way to attack and destroy the discontent lying in the minds of millions. Through this legislation you merely drive genuine, embittered dissatisfaction underground.

Why and how do you consider our demand a danger to strength and sovereignty?

Before answering, we must be clear what sovereignty means. What do we have in mind when we use the word? The Preamble to the Constitution says political sovereignty rests with the people. Legal power or sovereignty is distributed between the central federal structure and the state governments within the federation.

Why not view our programme as an effort to give sovereign state institutions greater and more effective authority? What would be wrong with that approach? Why assume that asking for Dravidastan cuts the root of sovereignty? Sovereignty does not reside entirely in one institution; do we not possess a federal system?

As many political thinkers have pointed out, India is immense, indeed often described as a subcontinent. Because it contains varied outlooks, traditions, and histories, an iron-cage unitary system is impossible. That is why the framers established a federation rather than a unitary state.

My grievance, supported by Praja Socialist member Gurupadaswamy and others, is that the federation's operation over these thirteen years has broken the states' spirits. They may not stand beside me, but they recognise that states are rapidly becoming bodies that merely receive grants. States feel pushed into the background, and naturally desire greater power.

Add disparities in regional development and the language problem. In those circumstances, do you consider it unnatural for people like me to become disappointed and think of separation?

Meet us halfway. Tell us, "You may go this far but no further." Yet when you meet us there, you must offer a proper answer for difficulties created not by us but by administering the Constitution in a way that harms the states.

### Images 13-15

Did the West Bengal and Central Governments not have to go to the Supreme Court over coal mines? The Law Minister comes from West Bengal. Are Bengalis fully satisfied? As law-abiding people, they will obey the Supreme Court's judgment.

Were my friend Bhupesh Gupta not a Communist, he would have been foremost in arguing for West Bengal's rights. I bow to the national feeling of Bengalis.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** I fought for those rights here; Dr B. C. Roy knows it.

**Anna:** But the battle was lost. I am sorry. My point is that the states are becoming increasingly dispirited. This strengthens the demand that the Union Government recognise the need to re-examine and freshly evaluate the Constitution.

My view has the support of a prominent man with the power to leave and re-enter the Cabinet whenever he wishes. I refer to T. T. Krishnamachari, Minister for Economic and Defence Coordination. Speaking on 8 September 1962 at a New Delhi forum established in memory of the late Feroze Gandhi, he said, as one of the Constitution's framers, that they had failed to include a provision requiring its review every ten years. He added that public opinion must gather behind such a review.

Regard me as one of the guardians of public opinion and come with me. Let us discover the states' outlook.

I do not wish to embarrass any ruling-party member, so I shall name no one. Many members of the ruling party in Tamil Nadu may swear that India is one and indivisible and pledge themselves to its sovereignty and territorial integrity. But whenever one of their proposals is ignored, a scheme they advocate is not adopted, or the required funds are not allocated, they remember me and say, "That is why Annadurai demands separation."

Deny Salem a steel plant and I arise. Deny development to Tuticorin and the DMK appears there.

The DMK should therefore be recognised as the spearhead of opposition to attempts to turn the federation into a unitary state.

You are the elders of Parliament. Why drive this problem into the wilderness? Raise it to a higher plane in the political arena. Make the federation a genuine federation.

Some members will ask, "But do you not speak of separation?" Bhupesh Gupta calls it unthinkable. Even if others do not, he ought to know that the Soviet Constitution grants the right to secede, yet no one there cries that sovereignty is endangered.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** He seems to take only the bad things from the Soviet Union, not the good.

**Anna:** I wish to tell him that merely demanding separation does not endanger sovereignty.

Even assuming our propaganda could endanger it, what should a democratic governing party do? Should it not go to the people? Do you not say that sovereignty rests with them? The people established the Constitution. You must appeal to the people, the source of political rights. I approach them with confidence.

I ask ruling-party members to tell their Government: "We possess the ability and strength to explain the issue to the public and campaign against Anna."

Why surrender your right?

As ruling-party members and responsible public servants, do not stand between the Government, us, and the people. If Annadurai conducts separatist propaganda, advise the Government: "We understand its absurdity and shall make the people understand it through our campaign." As a democrat, I ask this House to show ordinary people some respect. Do not imagine that anyone can deceive them. An ordinary person may not be highly educated or possess specialised legal knowledge, but...

## Part 2

### Images 1-3

...he possesses abundant common sense. He knows how to distinguish butter from lime.

By introducing this Bill, you effectively pass a resolution declaring that you have no faith in the common sense of all the country's people. Why not leave the issue to them?

Do not imagine that I and the handful of party members one can count on one's fingers can deceive the public and lead it astray. In the other House, the Law Minister offered an argument fit only to amuse schoolchildren.

**N. Sri Rama Reddy:** A misleading line of argument.

**Anna:** Mine?

**N. Sri Rama Reddy:** Your argument is capable of leading people astray.

**Akbar Ali Khan:** I ask Annadurai to consider how communal sentiments operated in India's history and how appeals issued in their name captured and led the people.

**Santosh Kumar Basu:** I wish to ask Annadurai, who has presented this issue so skilfully, another question. Is it not true that the demand for Pakistan arose only after the cry that the Central Government possessed excessive authority and that the provinces required greater powers?

**Anna:** Mr Deputy Chairman, after I have explained what I wish to say, if you kindly grant me a little more time, I shall try to answer that.

**Deputy Chairman:** You may take a little more time.

**Anna:** I was saying that, so long as sovereignty rests with the people, they must be the qualified authority empowered to decide every issue. As I have explained, the federal structure has been damaged to a certain extent and is moving toward a unitary system. The demand for separation must therefore be viewed together with the dissatisfaction arising in other states.

Even if our propaganda is assumed dangerous, ruling-party members should meet it with counter-propaganda. Even if they surrender their own right and leave it to the Government and executive, I ask the House to consider whether curtailing fundamental rights is truly necessary to campaign against and answer us.

I know very well that fundamental rights are not beyond restriction. Limitations exist.

**Akbar Ali Khan:** Quite true.

**Anna:** Parliament has every authority to limit those rights, indeed all rights. These are easily understood matters requiring little effort. But some effort is needed to understand that rights, not restrictions, are the important things. That is why our Constitution states so clearly that restrictions upon rights must be imposed for reasonable cause.

My humble submission is that this restriction has no reasonable cause. First, you have not analysed the other side's case. Second, you have not understood us. Third, you have offered us no alternative programme. Fourth, you do not consider the people worthy of trust.

This may not be a proper legal argument, but politically the restriction you introduce lacks sufficient cause.

When discussing fundamental rights in the other House, the Law Minister offered a curious argument: "If fundamental rights had been exercised in their entirety, some people would have used them even to welcome the Chinese." He added sentiments that some ruling-party members, especially those from West Bengal, might share.

I need not concern myself with that. But why should the Law Minister or any minister underestimate the public's ability to understand and judge? If anyone mounted a platform and declared, "We welcome the Chinese," would the people merely watch in silence?

Our people may not have studied the Constitution's parts and clauses, but they can distinguish good from evil. That is why, when the call for the freedom struggle came, they stood in the front rank despite the bondage imposed by a powerful empire.

### Images 4-6

Do not lose faith in the people. What the Law Minister said in the other House about fundamental rights was not only far from the truth but lacked deep thought.

Restrictions may be introduced, as I have said, but only after considering whether extraordinary circumstances exist that make Parliament's intervention imperative.

I believe it was in the Motilal Nehru Committee in 1928 that Jawaharlal Nehru stated very clearly: "It is not enough that we obtain our fundamental rights; we must assure our people that those rights will not be taken away under any circumstances."

Note my words carefully, Mr Deputy Chairman. Through you, I ask the members of the House to note them: "under any circumstances."

We may since have become weaker; I can understand that. But has any extraordinary situation arisen that warrants this restriction? No. Arguments have been made that the DMK may so far have remained within the law but possesses a mischievous and dangerous tendency that must be crushed with an iron hand. I do not have time to discuss the place or meaning legal experts assign to the word "tendency." But Justice Patanjali Sastri, one of the finest legal authorities, said: "It may be better to allow some poisonous branches to flourish than, in trying to remove them, to cut down the tree itself and destroy its life force."

That is one judicial observation concerning fundamental rights and their restrictions. There are many decisions of the United States Supreme Court. We are not bound by them, but they illustrate the progressive liberalism found in democratic countries.

At one time, in New York as I recall, a new law required those wishing to become teachers to swear allegiance and loyalty to the Constitution and political institutions. The Governor of New York vetoed it, saying that restricting a fundamental right in this way was unnecessary. A teacher's duty, he argued, was to teach lessons, not display personal beliefs and emotions.

We should follow the progressive liberal tradition cultivated in other democracies, or at least shape our thinking accordingly. If instead you say, "We possess the power to destroy any opposition party: today the DMK, tomorrow the Communist Party, and the next day the Jana Sangh," then I say: you have the authority, use it. But remember where governments that sought power and domination by legal repression have gone and what resulted; I need not remind you.

Even today, on this side of the House, Bhupesh Gupta said that not only the DMK but also the Jana Sangh must be opposed because he considers it a communal organisation.

The Praja Socialists said the Communist Party is more dangerous than the DMK. We opposition parties therefore make matters convenient for the Government.

**Sheel Bhadra Yajee:** It applies to every party advocating separation in India, whether the Communist Party or the DMK.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** There is Mr Yajee, Mr Deputy Chairman. If all of us must unite to oppose someone, it should be Mr Yajee.

**Honourable A. K. Sen, Law Minister:** Mr Deputy Chairman, it has been said that I attributed this peace to the Defence of India Rules. I did not say that; I said they were partly responsible. Those rules were created for persons engaging in wrongful activities. I did not say they were directed at the DMK or any specified person. I said the Defence of India Act was partly responsible for this peace. What is wrong in that?

**Anna:** My criticism was that such a comment displayed no sympathetic feeling.

**A. K. Sen:** Some people wish to fish in troubled waters, and some engage in wrongful acts. Can we fail to see that or blindfold ourselves? But the honourable member need not regard the cap as fitting his own head. I referred neither to him nor his party.

**Deputy Chairman:** Annadurai, please continue. I believe the matter has now been clarified.

**Anna:** If the ruling party acquires a taste for restricting speech, it may target the DMK today, but what guarantee is there that other parties will not be targeted tomorrow? The ruling party need not argue for that possibility; we argue for it ourselves.

### Images 7-9

The Praja Socialists argue for eliminating the Communists; the Communist Party argues for suppressing the Jana Sangh. The more this grows, the more the ruling party has cause to celebrate.

I therefore ask members to consider this as a question of curtailing fundamental rights. Let ruling-party members at least say that they can firmly counter our propaganda. Let them come forward to answer us through counter-propaganda and oppose the Bill completely for that reason.

My friend Bhupesh Gupta said he accepted the Bill in principle, while another friend, Gurupadaswamy, said he accepted its broad purpose.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** Its broad political purpose...

**Anna:** That means they recognise the consequences of restricting rights. I therefore ask you to consider the consequences of such a law, not merely the party against which it is directed.

If ruling-party members possess confidence in themselves and the people, they should recognise that a democratic society must not curtail the rights to think and express thought.

Discovering and communicating truth on matters of great importance is among the foremost purposes of society and government. It is possible only through unrestrained discussion. As Bagehot said, when violence is enlisted on one side, it becomes impossible to determine whether it supports truth or falsehood. Truth then loses every natural advantage in the battle of ideas.

Do not create peace through violence. Speak the language of the heart and awaken friendship. I ask ruling-party members to stand for fundamental rights.

Preserve your right to explain ideas to the public. Seek no law that abolishes the right to think and express disagreement.

Finally, Mr N. Sri Rama Reddy said my philosophical argument was misleading.

**N. Sri Rama Reddy:** An argument that leads people astray.

**Anna:** No member should consent to be led astray, and I possess no ability to mislead anyone. I first thought he meant my philosophy itself was mistaken, because I care greatly that it should remain unsullied. As to the accusation that I lead others astray, I say only that I am not powerful enough to do so.

**Pannalal Saraogi:** Mr Deputy Chairman, the honourable member is misleading the people of his State; that is why this Bill is being enacted.

**A member:** The fear is that you will mislead the people.

**Akbar Ali Khan:** On communal grounds...

**Deputy Chairman:** Very well, Annadurai, please conclude. He says you are misleading the people of your State and that this law is therefore necessary.

**Pannalal Saraogi:** You excite the people's thoughts and emotions, and they dance accordingly. That is why the Bill is being enacted.

**Deputy Chairman:** They fear they too may be made to dance.

**Anna:** Mr Deputy Chairman, that statement appears to underestimate the ability and strength of the Madras Congress.

**Santosh Kumar Basu:** My friend said not to use violent force. But has the DMK entirely renounced violence, or is it unconnected with those who follow that course? Have you always rejected violence?

**Anna:** Certainly. We have repeatedly stressed that we are a party that acts within the law.

**Basu:** Even though railway stations and nameboards were burned...

**Anna:** Not railway stations, but the Constitution. My respected friend mistakenly treats Dravidar Kazhagam actions as Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam actions. That is the trouble.

I also tell the House this: through the Bill you may prevent the DMK from contesting elections, but the Dravidar Kazhagam is not an electoral party, so this law will not affect it.

**Basu:** Our concern is not whether it is the DK or DMK; we are considering a law applicable to everyone.

### Images 10-12

**Vajpayee:** But I believe the DK is allied with Congress.

**Anna:** Congress must think about that.

My point, Mr Deputy Chairman, is this. I appeal to ruling-party members: tell the Government this Bill is unnecessary, contrary to democracy, and destructive of a fundamental right.

I do not mean the fundamental right contained in the Constitution. I mean the fundamental right of Congress members. They are given no part in the issue and are told to stand aside.

The Bill says: "Annadurai must be opposed and answered. You have failed, so I am coming; let me enter." I hold the Madras Congress in high regard, but this resembles passing a vote of no confidence in its ability and strength.

I respect them; you diminish their importance. That is the trouble.

You appear to believe they cannot answer us through counter-propaganda. That is the regrettable position.

I therefore appeal to ruling-party members: tell the Government, "We are here, brave and strong, to fight divisive tendencies. We shall take care of Annadurai. Leave us to it. He is a weakling; one look and one forceful word will be enough to crush the fellow."

Tell your party and Government this and withdraw the Bill. Otherwise, if it enters the statute book, people will think not only now but forever that a situation arose in India in which the Constitution itself had to be amended to answer a small party, or, to use my friend Bhupesh Gupta's words, one solitary man.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** No, no. I did not say that.

**Akbar Ali Khan:** I assure Annadurai that this is not introduced for one man or one party. The Bill opposes separatist tendencies wherever they exist, in Punjab, Madras, or any other part of the country. It also takes account of the communal feelings present over the last thirty years.

**Bhupesh Gupta:** Mr Deputy Chairman, I did not call him a solitary, isolated man.

**Anna:** I accepted his words gratefully, not critically.

As to the Honourable Akbar Ali Khan's statement, I understand the hesitation with which he presented his argument. I would say that the Bill is aimed not only at the DMK but at some others too. I am concerned with the party to which I belong; representatives of those others would have spoken similarly if present. But I need not concern myself with people who have drowned such an ideal for selfish gain. My purpose is to explain the DMK's position. The Bill may target many others, but you have seen the daily and weekly newspapers. Attend political platform speeches and you will hear them repeatedly point to "the wicked DMK."

Because the DMK has been singled out, I have answered some of the charges made.

Finally, in the name of democracy and political civility, I appeal to the member who introduced the Bill: possess unwavering faith in the people's ability to reject evil and abandon it. If he cannot agree, I ask him at least to postpone it in view of the emergency, for contentious issues ought to be kept in the background and not debated now.

If the member cannot accept even this appeal, Mr Deputy Chairman, permit me to record my condemnation of and opposition to the ruling party's methods, actions, and Bill. Thank you. Greetings.

---NOTES---
The source says Anna originally spoke in English and presents this Tamil text as a translation. The final bilingual document will therefore preserve the Tamil source verbatim and provide a fresh English rendering rather than claim to reconstruct Anna's exact original wording.

The corrected OCR originally read `25-17-1963`; direct inspection of Part 1 Image 1 confirms the printed date as `25-1-1963`, which has been corrected in the source and recorded in `ocr_concerns.csv`.

`அரசுரிமை` is rendered as “sovereignty” and `வல்லமையடைமை` as “strength” or “sovereign power” according to context. `பிரிவினை` is rendered as “separation,” “secession,” or “separatist” according to grammatical use.

The intervention names and date were verified against the Rajya Sabha Official Debates for 25 January 1963: <https://rsdebate.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/549556/2/ID_41_25011963_31_p4848_p4952_29.pdf>. The record identifies N. Sri Rama Reddy, Bhaurao Dewaji Khobragade, Sheel Bhadra Yajee, and Pannalal Saraogi.
